<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Feodor Chaliapin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1873">1873</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Fyodor Ivanovich Shalyapin, Fyodor Shalyapin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Feodor Chaliapin was a Russian bass singer, born into a peasant family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> in 1873, who is often credited with establishing the tradition of naturalistic acting in opera. After success as Mephistopheles at the Mamontov Private Opera, he sang at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>'s Bolshoi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> from 1899 to 1914, and the title role of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modest-mussorgsky">Mussorgsky</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a> became his signature part. From 1901 he sang abroad, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-scala">La Scala</a> and later the Metropolitan Opera, and his advocacy made Russian operas such as Khovanshchina and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-svyatoslavich">Prince Igor</a> well known in the West. He remained outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> after 1921, settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and died there in 1938; his remains were moved to Moscow in 1984.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/feodor-chaliapin.jpg" alt="Feodor Chaliapin" width="330" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (Bibliothèque nationale et universitaire de Strasbourg, France) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Feodor_Chaliapin_signed.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The singer <b>Feodor Chaliapin</b> made his own version, with changed words and further verses, and dedicated it to his future wife.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dark-eyes">Dark Eyes</a></li><li><blockquote>The Russian bass <b>Feodor Chaliapin</b> sang it and recorded it in the early days of recording, for His Master's Voice and Victor.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kalinka">Kalinka</a></li><li><blockquote>The bass <b>Feodor Chaliapin</b> made it famous, in an arrangement by Feodor Koenemann; his version, released several times (1922, 1927, 1936), became one of Russia's most popular songs, and it has been a concert favourite of bass singers ever since.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-song-of-the-volga-boatmen">The Song of the Volga Boatmen</a></li></ul></section>
